      <article id="scene-archives" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1997 through to the 2000s · The websites</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Defacto2 <small>and The Scene Archives</small></h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p class="small text-body-secondary">* Links to an incomplete recreation of the original website.</p>
          <p>
            The genesis for this website began decades ago in the middle of 1997. 
            Originally known as <strong><a href="/wayback/defacto2-from-1998-september-8/archive.html" rel="nofollow">The Scene Archives</a>*</strong>,
            the site started out as a subsection of the art group Defacto&nbsp;2&nbsp;(<em>defacto2.org</em>).
          </p>
          <p>
            Months later in October, a gossip and news section known as <strong><a href="/wayback/defacto2-from-1998-september-8/news.html" rel="nofollow">The Scene News</a>*</strong> got added to the website. 
            And in April of 1998, the domain name <em>defacto2.net</em> was registered.
            The move to a new domain reflected the change of the Defacto 2 brand from a Scene group or <u>org</u>anization to a <u>net</u>work of websites.
            And this sort of change wasn't unusual, Google didn't exist, domain squatting was uncommon, and search engine optimization was still an abstract idea.
          </p>
          <p>
            Inspiration for The Scene News came from <strong><a href="/wayback/scenelink-from-1998-june-25/index.html" rel="nofollow">SceneLink</a>*</strong>, 
            a well-designed web publication by <a href="/f/a42815">some students</a> of Yale University that covered The Scene in a broader sense, not just online piracy but also art and the online underground.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Scene Archives got heavily influenced by sites like <strong><a href="http://zakalwe.fi/~shd/texts/imr/zimr.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">In Medias Res</a>*</strong>, 
            a fantastic resource that, for a few years in the late 1990s, went around collecting interviews with active and retired members of The Scene on the Commodore 64 and Amiga microcomputers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Another inspiration was the <strong><a href="https://archaic-ruins.lngn.net/features/damaged/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Damaged Cybernetics</a>*</strong> group page that covered gray area information on various topics that were not explicitly wares.
            It showed there was a ready interest in other underground areas and even for redundant hardware and software, thanks to the new field of emulation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The history emphasis of the Scene with PC wares first came about by a person named Toast, a Canadian member of <a href="/g/razor-1911">Razor&nbsp;1911</a>.
            For years, he collected and organized thousands of text information files from deceased groups on the PC.
            It resulted in the <strong><a href="/f/a11dc">Toast's Complation of .NFO Files!</a></strong>, aka <strong>Scene History Archive NFO pack</strong>. 
            However, it was his 1995 website (<em class="text-nowrap">www.uoguelph.ca/~shost/toast.html</em>) 
            that was the first to compile and document the history of the PC scene and put it on the Internet.
          </p>
          <p>
            While Toast's collecting focused on NFO texts and BBS user logs, The Scene Archive wanted to cover a broader collection of items and digital artifacts.
            Looking back, the site was themed into a few sections including, 
            dedicated pages for some leading groups and bulletin boards;
            <em>Cracktros galore</em> page with a collection of hundred-plus cracktros;
            an <em>e-mags</em> page for the various PC Scene electronic magazines;
            the <em>scene bitchin'</em>, aka the drama files section; 
            and a modest collection of NFOs for online viewing.
          </p>
          <p>
            At some stage in the early 2000s, the other parts of the <em>defacto2.net</em> website were retired and archived leaving only The Scene Archives as the active subject.
            And a decision was made to rebrand The Scene Archives collection as <strong>Defacto2</strong>, to match the years old and established domain name.
          </p>
        </div>

